WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:01.857
There's no place to be
right now but in it.

00:00:01.857 --> 00:00:02.940
But fry the pigs?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.773
If they attack --

00:00:03.773 --> 00:00:04.606
Dr. King --

00:00:04.606 --> 00:00:05.640
Is dead!

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.772
There's no one in the Black Panther Party

00:00:07.772 --> 00:00:09.071
who would have spoken to the chairman

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:10.179
of the party that way.

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:11.550
Period.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.240
So that's No. 1.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.720
My name is Elaine Brown.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.498
I am a former leader of
the Black Panther Party

00:00:17.498 --> 00:00:21.240
in the years 1974 to 1977.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.098
I've been asked to take a look

00:00:23.098 --> 00:00:24.922
at some Hollywood films

00:00:24.922 --> 00:00:26.940
where the Black Panther Party is depicted

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.903
and to give my thoughts and
a review of those films.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.900
Shut that blind, man.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.130
And get your white a--
away from that window.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.049
Don't you know we in a war here?

00:00:41.049 --> 00:00:42.765
Hey, man, he's cool. He's
cool. He's one of us.

00:00:42.765 --> 00:00:44.320
Let me tell you about us.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.610
Everybody in America loves "Forrest Gump."

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.830
And so the shame of it is

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.958
that this one encounter
that Forrest Gump has

00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:55.638
is with this insane cult group of people

00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.900
that doesn't like white people.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.900
Well, of course it was a caricature,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.370
and it was certainly demeaning,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:03.970
not to count inaccurate.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.100
Well, nobody walked
around wearing that stuff.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.160
Every once in a while
we'd have those rallies

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.795
in the early days where people would wear,

00:01:10.795 --> 00:01:12.360
you know, the black leather.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.630
That was our sort of uniform.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.308
But nobody would be
walking around the office

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:17.508
with that clothing on.

00:01:17.508 --> 00:01:20.420
The Black Panthers are
against the war in Vietnam.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.156
Yes! We are against any war

00:01:22.156 --> 00:01:24.187
where Black soldiers are
sent to the front line

00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:26.260
to die for a country that hates them.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.526
The Vietnam War was launched in 1965

00:01:29.526 --> 00:01:30.917
by Lyndon Johnson.

00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:34.160
The party itself started in 1966.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.224
We were the only Black organization

00:01:36.224 --> 00:01:38.650
that was actually a part
of the antiwar movement.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.250
But then in my particular case, in 1970,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.691
I visited Hanoi and other places

00:01:43.691 --> 00:01:45.941
and did a radio interview

00:01:45.941 --> 00:01:48.430
with a figure called Hanoi Hannah.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.060
And we urged our Black brothers

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.000
to throw down their weapons and come home.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.285
Sorry I had a fight

00:01:56.285 --> 00:01:58.538
in the middle of your Black Panther party.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.665
So, to make light of it,

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:05.398
to make a joke out of us,

00:02:05.398 --> 00:02:07.542
then we become a caricature and a joke.

00:02:07.542 --> 00:02:11.680
There's nothing you can say
about it that's good at all,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.800
as to what was the Black Panther Party

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.610
and any depiction of it.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.893
And if you were to rank
it, which, you know,

00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:20.674
is just so typically
American, Western thinking,

00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:23.515
that everything has to be ranked,

00:02:23.515 --> 00:02:24.640
but if you were to rank it,

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.780
I don't think it's on the chart.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.850
It would be below one.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.490
There's no place to be
right now but in it.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:30.627
But fry the pigs?

00:02:30.627 --> 00:02:31.461
If they attack --

00:02:31.461 --> 00:02:32.294
Dr. King --

00:02:32.294 --> 00:02:33.127
Is dead!

00:02:33.127 --> 00:02:34.740
Bobby was very dynamic.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.062
Bobby Seale was the chairman

00:02:36.062 --> 00:02:38.310
and cofounder of the Black Panther Party.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.405
But I can tell you this:

00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:41.527
There's no one in the Black Panther Party

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:42.768
who would have spoken to the chairman

00:02:42.768 --> 00:02:43.926
of the party that way.

00:02:43.926 --> 00:02:45.290
Period.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.656
So that's No. 1.

00:02:46.656 --> 00:02:49.210
But I can't imagine anybody,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.830
any sister or brother,
telling the chairman

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.923
of the Black Panther Party
what to do or not do.

00:02:57.264 --> 00:02:59.033
I said it would be impossible for me

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:02.380
to care any less what you are tired of

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.570
and I demand to cross-examine the witness.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.788
And the fact that he would have spoken out

00:03:07.788 --> 00:03:10.410
in this style, it would
have been absolutely true.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.943
There's no way you would have told Bobby

00:03:11.943 --> 00:03:13.510
not to speak up in the courtroom,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.168
because that was almost the style

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:16.800
of the Black Panther Party.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.390
And not for arbitrary reasons.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.600
Don't be afraid to speak.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.793
Don't be afraid to not follow
their rules and so forth.

00:03:27.776 --> 00:03:30.609
[chains rustling]

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:34.920
We were pretty pissed off about it

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.600
when Bobby was gagged and bound.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.850
He was treated so incredibly
differently than the others.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.032
I thought it wasn't a bad film, as a film,

00:03:42.032 --> 00:03:45.680
because it was more in
tune with the times.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.685
We just need to make sure
that Nixon Black power

00:03:48.685 --> 00:03:50.993
doesn't equate Nixon
with the Black Panthers.

00:03:50.993 --> 00:03:51.950
Have you lost your mind, man?

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:53.620
Did you read Hoover's last memo on that?

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:54.480
Ehrlichman: That's my point.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.820
Goddamn terrifying.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.111
No, no, no. I gave him the green light

00:03:57.111 --> 00:03:58.379
to gut those sons of bitches.

00:03:58.379 --> 00:03:59.212
Ehrlichman: Good, good.

00:03:59.212 --> 00:04:00.570
I had forgotten about "The Butler."

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.375
I had written that out of my memory,

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:03.666
because that was another
one of those films

00:04:03.666 --> 00:04:05.210
I refuse to see.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.927
Why don't we just put Aunt
Jemima back on the pancake box?

00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:11.430
Or Uncle Ben back on
the rice box, you know?

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.680
This time, we take a stand
against these injustices

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.750
that have plagued our community.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.000
They take one of ours,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.141
we taking two of theirs.

00:04:20.141 --> 00:04:22.130
"If they kill one of us,
we kill two of them."

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.336
I mean, this kind of
simple-minded rhetoric,

00:04:25.336 --> 00:04:27.880
this is not something
anybody would have ever said.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.220
Why would we never have said it?

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.810
Because it didn't make any sense.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.240
That didn't mean we
wouldn't defend ourselves,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.249
but nobody's going out and saying,

00:04:35.249 --> 00:04:38.050
"Well, we gonna kill two of
them for every one they kill,"

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:38.960
all this kind of stuff.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.832
And we're not gonna allow the police

00:04:40.832 --> 00:04:42.400
to come to our communities
and shoot people.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.260
We will move on them, and we will do that,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.207
but that wasn't the essential thing.

00:04:46.207 --> 00:04:47.525
We ain't seen this boy --

00:04:47.525 --> 00:04:49.127
I'm gonna snatch the life out of you, boy!

00:04:49.127 --> 00:04:50.090
Now everybody just sit down.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.056
I'm sorry, Mr. Butler,

00:04:51.056 --> 00:04:52.372
I didn't mean to make fun of your hero.

00:04:52.372 --> 00:04:54.022
[slaps]

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.997
Everything you are and
everything you have is

00:04:58.997 --> 00:04:59.830
because of that butler.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.313
Now, this wouldn't have happened

00:05:01.313 --> 00:05:04.445
in anybody's universe, period.

00:05:04.445 --> 00:05:06.262
Period. You hear me?

00:05:06.262 --> 00:05:09.730
The idea that

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:10.660
this woman,

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.330
Oprah,

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.440
can play a role

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.170
where she slaps a member
of the Black Panther Party

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.690
gives Hollywood and white
people such comfort,

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.070
knowing, "This isn't the
way Black people are.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.683
We don't like them either.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.600
Even now we don't like them.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.670
We don't even like what they stood for.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:31.630
They were wrong.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:32.610
They were terrorists.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.000
They were killers.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.199
This man was a good solid brother

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:38.824
who took care of his family

00:05:38.824 --> 00:05:40.540
and didn't bother anybody,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.670
and he should have been respected

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.228
by his disrespectful son

00:05:45.228 --> 00:05:47.320
who joins the Black Panther Party."

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.847
It's just so shameful

00:05:49.847 --> 00:05:53.661
to try to show and to embrace the idea

00:05:53.661 --> 00:05:56.120
of the good Black versus the bad Black.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.337
And in this case, the bad
Black, what's the worst?

00:05:59.337 --> 00:06:00.770
Oh, Black Panthers.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.270
So, the point is,

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.270
there are so many
inaccuracies in this concept,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:07.411
not to count that the boy of that man

00:06:07.411 --> 00:06:09.520
was never in the Black Panther Party.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.742
But the main purpose of that stupid film

00:06:13.742 --> 00:06:18.740
was to elevate the role
of the good servile Negro.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.166
Overall, pretty bad.

00:06:20.166 --> 00:06:21.478
I pledge to develop...

00:06:21.478 --> 00:06:23.012
Crowd: I pledge to develop...

00:06:23.012 --> 00:06:25.557
Hampton: My mind and body to
the greatest extent possible.

00:06:25.557 --> 00:06:28.589
Crowd: My mind and body to
the greatest extent possible.

00:06:28.589 --> 00:06:30.190
I will learn all that I can.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.440
The Black Panther Party
Breakfast for Children Program

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.550
was not the centerpiece of
the Black Panther Party,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.480
first of all.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.942
It was a tool -- the
ideological underpinning

00:06:39.942 --> 00:06:42.251
was to bring people together

00:06:42.251 --> 00:06:46.110
and to recognize that
they had a right to eat.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.928
You have a right to food.

00:06:47.928 --> 00:06:49.890
And we still see that today.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.870
We talked about the right to medical care.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.789
We talked the right to food, clothing,

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:58.780
all these things that, right
now, are still a problem.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.655
So, as a Black person,

00:07:00.655 --> 00:07:03.613
when you look at the
situation then as now,

00:07:03.613 --> 00:07:05.600
you find that we have the highest rate

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.950
of prostate and breast
cancer deaths in America.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.872
And then we said, well, people are dying

00:07:10.872 --> 00:07:12.555
because they have no access

00:07:12.555 --> 00:07:14.705
to even the most minimal medical care.

00:07:14.705 --> 00:07:17.075
And so we said, we have to put clinics up,

00:07:17.075 --> 00:07:20.104
we have to show people
that you have a human right

00:07:20.104 --> 00:07:21.736
to access to healthcare.

00:07:21.736 --> 00:07:23.472
And the reason we said "free"

00:07:23.472 --> 00:07:25.738
is because we wanted
people to conceptualize

00:07:25.738 --> 00:07:28.971
the idea that in a humane society,

00:07:28.971 --> 00:07:31.012
we're not going to make people pay

00:07:31.012 --> 00:07:34.060
for those things that keep you alive.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.427
So I think that Fred
Hampton recognized that,

00:07:36.427 --> 00:07:39.558
because he was ideologically
very strong, as I knew him.

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:41.670
Unlike the director of this film

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.490
and his friend, Ryan Coogler,

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:44.830
I knew Fred Hampton.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.687
And I knew that he was very
much ideologically aligned

00:07:48.687 --> 00:07:52.080
and understood the purpose
of all of these programs.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.848
This is why the Chicago police

00:07:54.848 --> 00:07:57.180
would come in and assault our clinics.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.505
You know, there in Chicago,

00:07:58.505 --> 00:08:00.280
they took some very heavy hits

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.220
on breakfast programs and so forth.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.411
Because the fear of J. Edgar Hoover,

00:08:05.411 --> 00:08:07.387
which he stated openly,

00:08:07.387 --> 00:08:08.903
it wasn't that we had guns.

00:08:08.903 --> 00:08:10.778
Nobody was afraid of our guns.

00:08:10.778 --> 00:08:14.290
What do we have compared
to the Chicago PD?

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:17.250
Or the Illinois State
Police that murdered Fred?

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.980
Or the United States Army,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.420
or the United States Navy,

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.100
or the United States Marine Corps,

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.717
or the United States Air Force,

00:08:23.717 --> 00:08:26.625
or the CIA, or the FBI, or the ATF,

00:08:26.625 --> 00:08:28.573
and all the other agencies
of this government

00:08:28.573 --> 00:08:30.000
that are armed to the teeth?

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.427
Now, you know the Black Panther Party

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:34.510
did not represent a threat
on the issue of guns.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.430
What was the threat that we represented?

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.743
We had an agenda to understand

00:08:38.743 --> 00:08:41.527
that the reason we remained
oppressed in America

00:08:41.527 --> 00:08:43.808
was because there was this fundamental

00:08:43.808 --> 00:08:47.175
400-year-old existing system

00:08:47.175 --> 00:08:50.510
that had oppressed us as
a group in the beginning,

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.152
and so, therefore, the only
way that we could be free

00:08:53.152 --> 00:08:56.360
was that this whole system
had to be dismantled.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:57.420
Now that's what we understood

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.910
and I absolutely know that
Fred Hampton understood.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:04.592
So, the limitation, the narrow, pathetic,

00:09:04.592 --> 00:09:07.210
two-dimensional characterization of Fred,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:10.290
oh, that was nothing compared
to who Fred Hampton was.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.598
To put Fred into any context

00:09:12.598 --> 00:09:14.806
with this piece of garbage,

00:09:14.806 --> 00:09:15.797
William O'Neal --

00:09:15.797 --> 00:09:18.630
and this guy was on screen
as much as Fred Hampton.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.935
So what you've done is make him

00:09:21.935 --> 00:09:24.060
like the other side of Fred Hampton.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.515
Like there's Fred Hampton,

00:09:25.515 --> 00:09:27.640
and then there's William O'Neal.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.137
William O'Neal had no such significance

00:09:30.137 --> 00:09:33.703
in the history of either
the party or Black people.

00:09:33.703 --> 00:09:37.580
And this is so insulting to
the history of Fred Hampton,

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.210
much less the Black Panther Party,

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.876
to equate these men

00:09:40.876 --> 00:09:44.217
as though they were two sides of the coin.

00:09:44.217 --> 00:09:46.779
You know, "Well, one turned
out this way, one..."

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:48.112
No!

00:09:48.112 --> 00:09:50.014
This was a fight for Black people

00:09:50.014 --> 00:09:51.022
and our liberation.

00:09:51.022 --> 00:09:53.060
If you had been respectful
of that history,

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.970
then you might've shown more respect

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:56.503
to not only Fred Hampton,

00:09:56.503 --> 00:09:58.094
but to the Black Panther Party.

00:09:58.094 --> 00:09:59.685
We want full-fledged membership

00:09:59.685 --> 00:10:01.032
in the Black Panther Party.

00:10:01.032 --> 00:10:02.881
And none of that, "OK, sugar.

00:10:02.881 --> 00:10:04.306
As long as you play the background,

00:10:04.306 --> 00:10:06.180
getting my beer and rubbing my feet"

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.019
bulls--- either. You dig?

00:10:08.019 --> 00:10:09.845
Those sisters standing
there looking tough,

00:10:09.845 --> 00:10:11.312
"We're gonna be in the party,"

00:10:11.312 --> 00:10:12.453
who the hell did that?

00:10:12.453 --> 00:10:14.120
It's just downright silly.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.100
Nobody did that.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.970
And to make the point
that women were equal,

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:21.710
I mean, it just wasn't
something that even came up.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:25.022
There were women in the Black
Panther Party from day one.

00:10:25.022 --> 00:10:28.350
Ericka Huggins was one of
the first members in LA.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.820
And when I joined, she was my captain.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.005
So we had women in leadership

00:10:33.005 --> 00:10:35.730
almost from the beginning,
that I can recall.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.070
I don't know where that's coming from.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.236
And it's kind of insulting

00:10:39.236 --> 00:10:41.536
to both the men in the party

00:10:41.536 --> 00:10:46.536
and to the party's fundamental
belief system and ideology.

00:10:46.771 --> 00:10:48.440
Absolutely not. We're
here to send a message.

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:49.808
The Black Panther Party for Self Defense

00:10:49.808 --> 00:10:51.770
calls upon the American people in general

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.298
and Black people in particular

00:10:53.298 --> 00:10:55.581
to take careful note of the
racist California legislature

00:10:55.581 --> 00:10:57.047
now considering the Mulford Act.

00:10:57.047 --> 00:10:58.565
So, this was the first and only time

00:10:58.565 --> 00:11:01.098
the National Rifle Association

00:11:01.098 --> 00:11:02.614
supported a gun-control bill.

00:11:02.614 --> 00:11:04.304
[laughs] Which is very funny.

00:11:04.304 --> 00:11:05.420
As soon as some Black people

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:06.795
started talking about carrying guns.

00:11:06.795 --> 00:11:09.830
And all this was, was what
is now called open carry.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.270
Because at that time in California,

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.785
you could carry a shotgun.

00:11:12.785 --> 00:11:14.300
That was part of our organizing.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.130
To say, "We have the right to bear arms."

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.400
You want to say white people
have the right to bear arms?

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.120
So as soon as we assume
that second amendment right,

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:24.710
as all of these
organizations like to claim,

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.610
"Oh, also, we got to have gun control."

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:28.960
So it was an impressive moment,

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:33.357
and a lot of people were shocked by it,

00:11:33.357 --> 00:11:36.600
that Black people, not
only that we had guns,

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.310
but we were not there as thugs.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:41.650
We were there with an ideological drive.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:42.791
As Huey Newton used to say,

00:11:42.791 --> 00:11:45.274
the gun is not necessarily revolutionary,

00:11:45.274 --> 00:11:47.167
because the police have guns.

00:11:47.167 --> 00:11:48.938
So we know it's not a revolutionary act.

00:11:48.938 --> 00:11:52.087
But in this case, it's the
ideology behind the gun,

00:11:52.087 --> 00:11:54.535
and that ideology is a championing

00:11:54.535 --> 00:11:59.080
of the right of self-defense,
under the larger agenda

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:01.481
of seeking liberation for Black people.

00:12:04.173 --> 00:12:07.861
[cameras flashing]

00:12:07.861 --> 00:12:10.065
I love Jean Seberg.

00:12:10.065 --> 00:12:12.880
And I knew her very well. Very well.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.615
I saw pieces of this stupid movie,

00:12:15.615 --> 00:12:19.625
but the scenes I saw
were just so inaccurate

00:12:19.625 --> 00:12:24.410
and so disrespectful of
Jean and who she was.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:26.188
Hollywood was a big supporter

00:12:26.188 --> 00:12:27.360
of the Black Panther Party.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.440
In our chapter in Southern California,

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.138
we knew every Hollywood star,

00:12:32.138 --> 00:12:33.345
because at that moment we were

00:12:33.345 --> 00:12:34.895
the flavor of the year. You know?

00:12:34.895 --> 00:12:36.136
And one of our biggest supporters

00:12:36.136 --> 00:12:37.644
was a guy named Bert Schneider,

00:12:37.644 --> 00:12:41.080
who made a film called "Easy Rider."

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.680
And, of course, Jane Fonda.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.598
So, the LA chapter,

00:12:44.598 --> 00:12:47.128
we were sort of assigned
to go and see her,

00:12:47.128 --> 00:12:48.912
because this was money that could come

00:12:48.912 --> 00:12:50.120
into the Black Panther Party.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.560
So that's why I know so
much about Jean Seberg.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.170
I remember when ultimately
my baby was born,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:58.360
she sent me a card and some
baby clothes, stuff like this.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.840
She was a really genuinely
decent and good person.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:03.718
If bringing them here raises their profile

00:13:03.718 --> 00:13:04.943
and it draws attention to the cause,

00:13:04.943 --> 00:13:05.993
then I've done my job.

00:13:05.993 --> 00:13:07.910
She said she wanted to support us.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.593
But she didn't want to be open about it,

00:13:09.593 --> 00:13:11.968
because she felt that if she were,

00:13:11.968 --> 00:13:14.410
that she would never get work in Hollywood

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:17.930
and that she would not be
able to support us, therefore.

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:22.923
And so she would give donations

00:13:22.923 --> 00:13:25.505
of $10,000 to $15,000, you know?

00:13:25.505 --> 00:13:27.620
Which would go to the Northern California,

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:30.510
up to Northern California
and be spent on, you know,

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.033
food, production of our
newspaper, what have you.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.663
They've tapped our phones in Los Angeles.

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.240
They're listening.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.777
I can hear these little
clicks on the line.

00:13:42.777 --> 00:13:45.008
I mean, we were all aware --

00:13:45.008 --> 00:13:46.983
we assumed phones were being tapped.

00:13:46.983 --> 00:13:48.510
Nobody was unconscious.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.648
But I don't think we understood

00:13:50.648 --> 00:13:52.728
the magnitude of what we were up against,

00:13:52.728 --> 00:13:54.730
which was the entire
United States government.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:56.946
And there was a paranoia that,

00:13:56.946 --> 00:13:57.829
if you want to call it that,

00:13:57.829 --> 00:13:59.470
but, you know, paranoias were being raised

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:01.111
that we were afraid of imaginary things.

00:14:01.111 --> 00:14:05.330
This was nothing to --
nothing imaginary in it.

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:06.970
But she definitely lost everything.

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:11.830
And their point was to discredit
the Black Panther Party.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.019
Jean was certainly, you know,

00:14:14.019 --> 00:14:16.590
collateral damage, a casualty.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:20.652
I don't think this film, in any way,

00:14:20.652 --> 00:14:23.093
gives credibility to Jean's commitment

00:14:23.093 --> 00:14:25.401
to the ideals that the party espoused.

00:14:25.401 --> 00:14:27.220
That's why I have
problems with these films.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.662
It's because that, even
though they get Black people

00:14:29.662 --> 00:14:31.803
to sort of take over the film,

00:14:31.803 --> 00:14:36.659
the Black Panther Party remains demonized,

00:14:36.659 --> 00:14:38.600
mischaracterized

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.553
in the public imagery
that Hollywood produces.

